          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>6</strong></td>
            <td>Automate</td>
            <td>Agent decides and acts</td>
            <td>Agent creates intermediate goals and decides execution steps; tools are the only hard boundary</td>
            <td>Claude Code given a high-level objective — it plans, edits, tests, and iterates without a predefined graph</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="Your SupportDesk API is at level 4–5">
      <p>
        The supervisor routes with forced <code>tool_choice</code> (level 4): all three specialist
        paths are predefined, the LLM just picks one. Each specialist then runs a ReAct loop (level 5):
        the tool set is bounded, turns are limited, loop detection is active. The system never reaches
        level 6 — no specialist invents new steps or calls tools outside its registered set.
        That is a deliberate design choice, not a limitation.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Choosing the right level</h3>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer → lower autonomy</th><th>Answer → higher autonomy</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>How predictable is the task?</td><td>Predictable, bounded steps</td><td>Unpredictable, exploratory</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What are the stakes if it fails?</td><td>High stakes, compliance, audit</td><td>Low stakes, easily reversed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Must decisions be explainable?</td><td>Every decision auditable</td><td>Black box acceptable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>How often does the task type change?</td><td>Stable, well-understood workflow</td><td>Evolving, hard to predefine</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Case studies: the same trade-off in production</h3>

    <div class="case-study-grid">
      <div class="case-study-card case-study-card--workflow">
        <div class="cs-header">
          <span class="cs-badge cs-badge--workflow">Agentic Workflow</span>
          <strong>Gemini — Meeting Notes</strong>
        </div>
        <p class="cs-goal"><strong>Goal:</strong> From a Google Meet call, generate a Google Doc with the meeting summary, decisions, and suggested next steps — then attach it to the calendar event and email it to key attendees.</p>
        <div class="cs-steps">
          <div class="cs-step">1. Listen to conversation</div>
          <div class="cs-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="cs-step">2. Generate structured Doc</div>
          <div class="cs-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="cs-step">3. Attach + email</div>
        </div>
        <p class="cs-why"><strong>Why not fully autonomous?</strong> It's a repeatable, bounded pipeline — capture → synthesise → publish. The same three steps run every time. An autonomous agent would be overkill and harder to debug when step 2 produces poor output.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="case-study-card case-study-card--auto">
        <div class="cs-header">
          <span class="cs-badge cs-badge--auto">Autonomous Agent</span>
          <strong>Claude Code — Coding tasks</strong>
        </div>
        <p class="cs-goal"><strong>Goal:</strong> Given a coding objective (fix a bug, implement a feature, refactor), drive all steps end-to-end: plan, edit files, run tests, interpret failures, iterate.</p>
        <div class="cs-steps">
          <div class="cs-step">Explore repo</div>
          <div class="cs-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="cs-step">Decide steps</div>
          <div class="cs-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="cs-step">Edit + test</div>
          <div class="cs-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="cs-step">Iterate</div>
        </div>
        <p class="cs-why"><strong>Why autonomous?</strong> You cannot predefine the workflow — the right sequence depends on what the agent discovers: repo structure, failing tests, unexpected errors. The engineer provides the goal and tools; the agent decides every intermediate step.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="Graduated autonomy in practice">
      <p>
        Most production systems use <strong>graduated autonomy</strong>: high control for
        critical operations, more autonomy for routine tasks, HITL for edge cases.
        Your SupportDesk API uses this pattern: the supervisor routes with high predictability
        (explicit intent → specialist), but each specialist has autonomy within its domain.
        Start constrained and add autonomy only when you have the eval coverage to detect regressions.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

  </div>

